{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795337224,"nonce":"dkatgbqGBP8LHOOd6JUoL6WrSBM=","title":"hybrid: a nested-loop function first entered from compiled code is never promoted to LLVM","message":"A physics-step shape stays at Cranelift speed for the whole run: an instance method with nested loops, called once per frame by a frame function that has already been compiled. The tier log shows `osr-transfer proposes findex=23 tier=llvm`, then `defer findex=23 tier=llvm reason=handed-off`, and no LLVM install follows. Found while closing 146013d, which covered the case where a later call reaches the interpreter. This is the case it noted as not reproduced.\n\nReproducer (Haxe 4.3.6, -hl): class World with `step()` (4096 Singles, 1000 repetitions, `a[i] = a[i] * 0.999 + b[i]`) and `frame(f)` that calls `step()` once f \u003e= 30. `main` calls `w.frame(f)` for f in 0...60 and times each call. Source: scratchpad/cc3/CCBench3.hx of the session that filed this; about 50 lines.\n\nns per element, Mac, default hybrid, one run:\n\n    frame 30..59, every call     3.4 - 3.7\n    same program, --jit-tier llvm        1.22\n\nCause: the postponement from e510c5f skips the ordinary promote after an OSR re-tier exit is published, for a function entered at most once from the interpreter. `World.frame` is compiled by its call count before `step` is first called, so `step` is entered once, through the stub bridge, and every later call is a direct call between compiled functions. Those tick nothing, so the postponed promote is never asked for again. `called_from_loop` does not see it either: the caller has no interpreter frame, and the nearest interpreted frame (main) has not passed 64 back-edges.\n\nThe stub bridge is where compiled code enters the interpreter and it already knows the compiled caller. A function entered from there has a caller that was compiled because it ran often, which is the same evidence the postponement already takes from an interpreted caller deep in a loop.\n\nFix direction: treat the first entry through the stub bridge as a re-entry in the hand-off ledger (5c04780b), so the promote goes ahead, or is paid if it was already postponed.\n\nNot covered: a function whose compiled callers reach it without ever going through the stub bridge, for example one whose Cranelift body was installed before any compiled caller was built. Not reproduced.\n\nRelated: 146013d (closed), e510c5f (closed).","files":null}]}